export const DATA_30: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-037", num: 37, title: "Đề 037", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Rise of Online Learning", paragraphs: ["Online learning has grown enormously over the past decade, changing how millions of students study around the world. In the early 2010s, only a small number of universities offered courses through the internet, and most people saw them as a minor addition to traditional classrooms. That view shifted quickly when health restrictions forced schools to close their doors and move lessons onto digital platforms almost overnight. Since then, enrollment in online courses has continued to climb, even after in-person classes resumed, showing that the shift was not simply a temporary response to an emergency.", "One of the clearest advantages of studying online is flexibility, since learners can watch lectures and complete assignments whenever their schedule allows. A factory worker in a small town, for example, might finish a certification course late at night after a long shift, something that would be impossible with a fixed classroom timetable. Online programs also tend to cost less than campus-based degrees, because institutions save money on buildings, transportation, and printed materials, and some of those savings are passed on to students. For people who live far from any college or who cannot travel due to family responsibilities, remote study is sometimes the only realistic path toward a qualification.", "Despite these benefits, many students struggle to stay motivated without the structure of a physical classroom. Surveys of online learners consistently report that procrastination and a sense of isolation are the biggest obstacles , more so than the course material itself. Technical problems add another layer of difficulty: a slow internet connection or an outdated computer can turn a simple video lecture into a frustrating experience. Some learners also miss the immediate feedback and casual conversation that happen naturally in a shared room, finding that typed messages on a forum are a poor substitute for a raised hand and a quick answer.", "In response to these problems, many universities now combine online material with occasional in-person sessions, a model often called blended or hybrid learning. Under this approach, students might watch recorded lectures at home but travel to campus for laboratory work, group projects, or exams that require closer supervision. Some institutions have also introduced virtual study groups and online tutoring hours, hoping to recreate a sense of community that pure video courses often lack. Early results suggest that students in blended programs report higher satisfaction than those in fully online courses, although they still value the convenience that remote study provides.", "Looking ahead, most experts agree that online learning is here to stay in some form, even as the exact balance between digital and physical classrooms continues to change. New tools such as interactive simulations and small-group video sessions are being tested to address the isolation that many students describe. Whatever shape the technology takes, the past several years have shown that education no longer has to happen in a single building at a fixed time of day."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The financial savings that online courses offer to distant learners", "How online education has grown and how schools address its problems", "The technical problems that make online learning impossible for everyone", "The complete replacement of physical classrooms by digital technology soon"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what caused a large increase in online enrollment?", options: ["An emergency closure of schools pushed lessons onto the internet", "Universities offered courses through the internet in the early 2010s", "Enrollment increased mainly after in-person classes fully resumed", "Students personally chose to abandon classrooms for online study"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Why might online study be the only realistic option for some learners, according to the passage?", options: ["Institutions save money on buildings and transportation costs instead", "Factory workers must complete their courses only during the daytime", "They live too far from a college to attend in person", "Campus-based degrees no longer offer any formal certification today"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge of online learning?", options: ["Higher tuition fees compared to traditional campus-based degree programs", "A feeling of isolation from other students during lessons", "Problems caused by a slow internet connection or outdated computer", "A lack of immediate feedback and casual conversation with teachers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"obstacles\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["disasters", "difficulties", "assignments", "obligations"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"recreate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["invent", "record", "rebuild", "recall"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["occasional in-person sessions", "a physical classroom", "online material", "the hybrid learning model"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about students in blended learning programs?", options: ["They are more satisfied because they no longer need computers", "Their higher satisfaction causes universities to add in-person sessions", "They want a mix of in-person structure and flexible access", "Universities save money by using recorded lectures at home"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the future of online learning, as expressed in the final paragraph?", options: ["Skeptical that any new technology can solve student isolation", "Cautiously optimistic that new tools can improve remote study", "Enthusiastic that physical classrooms will disappear completely soon", "Confident that online learning will fully replace campuses everywhere"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do universities offer in addition to recorded lectures under blended programs?", options: ["Virtual study groups and online tutoring hours for students", "Printed materials mailed directly to every enrolled student's home", "Weekly campus visits required for every single course lecture", "Laboratory work and group projects that need close supervision"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Robotic Revolution in the Strawberry Fields", paragraphs: ["For decades, strawberry farms have depended on armies of seasonal workers who bend over rows of low plants for hours at a time, picking each berry by hand because delicate fruit bruises easily under mechanical pressure. That reliance is now being tested by a shortage of available laborers in many farming regions, a gap that has pushed agricultural engineers to design machines capable of a task long considered too delicate for automation. Several companies have spent the past few years building robotic harvesters that use cameras and mechanical arms to pick strawberries with something close to human care, though whether they can fully replace human hands remains an open question.", "These machines rely on a combination of high-resolution cameras and artificial intelligence software that analyzes the color, size, and shape of each berry to judge whether it has reached the right stage of ripeness. Once a target fruit is identified, a soft robotic gripper, often made from silicone or a similarly flexible material, closes around the stem rather than the fruit itself, twisting or cutting it free without leaving bruises on the skin. Some prototypes can inspect and, if necessary, pick more than one plant at a time, moving along the rows on wheeled or tracked platforms guided by GPS and onboard mapping systems.", "Because the sensors never tire and the software can run around the clock, these harvesters may offer a consistency that human crews, working long shifts under a hot sun, cannot always match. Farm owners who have tested early models report that the machines tend to waste fewer berries, since the cameras can be calibrated to reject fruit that is slightly overripe or under-ripe with more consistency than a rushed human picker might apply. A further advantage, one that becomes clear only once a farm has struggled through a difficult hiring season, is that a robotic fleet does not disappear overnight if seasonal workers decide to seek better-paying jobs elsewhere.", "Robotic pickers are still, on the whole, slower and considerably more expensive than the labor they are meant to replace, despite years of refinement. A single advanced harvesting unit can cost as much as a small house, a price that only the largest commercial farms can currently justify, and even then the machines sometimes struggle on uneven or sloped ground where their wheels lose traction. Detecting ripeness under variable lighting, such as the dappled shade beneath leaves or the harsh glare of midday sun, also continues to confuse the cameras more often than manufacturers had originally hoped.", "In field trials conducted on farms in California, one early model needed nearly three times as long to harvest a row as an experienced human picker, although developers claim that later versions have narrowed that gap substantially . Engineers involved in these trials suggest that improvements in battery life and camera processing speed could eventually close much of the remaining distance, though they are careful not to promise a specific date. Farm managers who took part in the trials noted that the robots performed best on flat, evenly spaced rows, precisely the conditions that many older strawberry fields were never designed to provide.", "The prospect of widespread automation has understandably unsettled many farmworkers, some of whom fear that a technology marketed as a solution to labor shortages could just as easily become a replacement for the labor that remains. Labor advocates argue that any transition should include retraining programs, pointing out that machine operation and maintenance still require trained staff, even if far fewer than a full picking crew. Agricultural economists, for their part, tend to be more cautious, noting that similar predictions about automation eliminating farm jobs have circulated for other crops for decades without fully materializing.", "Whether robotic harvesters eventually dominate strawberry fields the way mechanical combines transformed grain farming a century ago remains uncertain, and much may depend on further drops in hardware costs as much as on any single technical breakthrough. What does seem clear, based on the pace of current investment, is that fully manual harvesting is unlikely to remain the only method available to strawberry growers for much longer."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Robotic arms use silicone grippers to avoid bruising strawberries", "Robotic harvesters have already proven cheaper and faster than pickers", "Robotic strawberry pickers show promise but still face major limits", "Automation will completely eliminate farm labor across all crops soon"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do robotic grippers avoid bruising strawberries?", options: ["They grip and cut the stem instead of touching the fruit", "They use cameras to judge the size and shape of each berry", "They squeeze the fruit gently until it releases from the plant", "They wait until the fruit falls naturally from the plant"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on the passage, why are robotic harvesters currently limited mainly to the largest farms?", options: ["Because the robots struggle on uneven or sloped ground surfaces", "Because only large farms can currently afford their high price", "Because small farms show no interest in new farm technology", "Because government regulations restrict robots to the largest farms"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the California field trial suggest about the consistency advantage described earlier in the passage?", options: ["The robot needed nearly three times as long to harvest a row", "Consistent picking made the robot three times faster than humans", "The advantage did not fully offset the robots' slower overall speed", "The trial proved that consistency advantages do not exist for robots"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge facing robotic strawberry harvesters?", options: ["Being universally more expensive than hiring human pickers instead", "Frequent mechanical breakdowns occurring during cold winter storage months", "Struggling on uneven or sloped ground where wheels lose traction", "Difficulty detecting ripeness under changing or uneven lighting conditions"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"calibrated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["adjusted", "perfected", "programmed", "celebrated"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"substantially\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["slightly", "considerably", "mechanically", "sustainably"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["several companies", "mechanical arms", "human hands", "robotic harvesters"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the agricultural economists' comment in the passage?", options: ["Robotic harvesters will never be widely adopted on strawberry farms", "Farm job losses caused economists to study automation in other crops", "Concerns about robots eliminating jobs may be exaggerated historically", "Labor advocates want retraining programs for machine operation and maintenance"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the future of robotic strawberry harvesting, as expressed in the final paragraph?", options: ["Dismissive of any possibility that robots will improve further", "Uncertain but inclined to think manual harvesting alone will not persist", "Alarmed that farmworkers will lose their jobs immediately", "Fully convinced that robots will replace all human pickers soon"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Hidden Cost of Fast Fashion", paragraphs: ["That a cotton T-shirt can retail for less than the price of a coffee says less about the efficiency of modern manufacturing than it does about costs that never appear on the receipt. Behind the discounted price tag lies a chain of environmental and human expenses — from water-intensive cultivation to underpaid labor — that consumers rarely see and retailers rarely advertise. While the fashion industry has long defended rapid production cycles as a democratizing force, offering runway-inspired styles to shoppers who could never afford couture, critics increasingly argue that this framing obscures a far less flattering picture of the supply chain that makes such prices possible.", "Cotton, still the fiber underlying a large share of fast-fashion garments, requires immense quantities of water to grow, particularly in regions where irrigation depends on diverting rivers or draining aquifers already under strain from other agricultural demands. A single T-shirt, according to some estimates, can require several thousand liters of water across its production, a figure that becomes more troubling once multiplied across the billions of garments manufactured annually. Although synthetic fibers such as polyester avoid much of this water burden, they introduce a different problem: shedding microplastic fibers with every wash, fibers that ultimately make their way into rivers, oceans, and even the food chain.", "Manufacturing itself has migrated over recent decades to countries where labor costs remain low, a shift that has undeniably created employment in regions with few alternatives. Wages in many garment-producing factories, however, often fail to keep pace with the cost of living in the very cities where those factories operate, leaving workers dependent on overtime hours simply to cover rent and food. Investigations following several factory disasters have revealed that safety standards, too, are frequently treated as negotiable when production deadlines tighten, a pattern that persists despite repeated pledges of reform from major retailers.", "Perhaps the least visible cost of all is what happens once a garment falls out of fashion, since the same rapid design cycles that make new clothing cheap also make old clothing disposable almost by design. Textile waste now accumulates at a scale that outpaces recycling infrastructure in most countries, with a significant share of discarded garments ending up incinerated, landfilled, or shipped to secondhand markets abroad that are themselves increasingly overwhelmed. Blended fabrics, which combine synthetic and natural fibers for durability or texture, compound the problem further, since separating those fibers for genuine recycling remains technically difficult and economically unrewarding for most processors.", "Consumers are not simply passive recipients of these dynamics, though the industry's marketing often invites them to think of low prices as an unqualified benefit. Surveys suggest that a growing number of shoppers express concern about the environmental and labor practices behind their clothing, yet purchasing habits have been slower to shift than stated attitudes would predict, a gap researchers attribute partly to the sheer convenience and affordability that fast fashion still offers relative to more sustainable alternatives. Some retailers have responded with recycling programs or \"conscious\" clothing lines, though critics note that these initiatives frequently affect a small fraction of total output.", "Policymakers in several regions have begun exploring measures such as extended producer responsibility, which would require manufacturers to bear some of the cost of collecting and processing discarded garments, though implementation has proven slower than advocates had hoped. Industry representatives, for their part, warn that overly strict regulation could simply push production toward countries with weaker environmental oversight rather than genuinely reducing the industry's footprint, an outcome that would undermine the very goals such policies are meant to achieve. Whether regulation can outpace the industry's ability to relocate production remains, at this stage, an open question.", "What emerges from these overlapping pressures is not a single villain but a system in which cheap prices, convenience, and consumer demand reinforce production practices that externalize costs onto workers, ecosystems, and future waste-management systems. Addressing that system, most researchers agree, will require coordinated pressure from consumers, regulators, and manufacturers simultaneously, since incremental change from any single actor has so far proven insufficient to alter the industry's fundamental economics."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the overall argument of the passage?", options: ["Cotton cultivation consumes far more water than synthetic fiber production", "Low retail prices conceal costs that current market and policy structures have yet to address", "Regulatory efforts have already succeeded in reducing the industry's environmental harm", "No single measure, including regulation or consumer choice, can ever reduce fast fashion's harm"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on the passage, why do blended fabrics present a greater long-term problem than pure cotton garments once discarded?", options: ["Separating their mixed fibers for recycling is difficult and unprofitable for processors", "They require several thousand liters of water to produce a single garment", "They are always incinerated immediately rather than sent to landfills", "They shed microplastic fibers only during the manufacturing stage"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What does the passage suggest is a risk of imposing stricter environmental regulation on fast-fashion manufacturers?", options: ["Workers already depend on overtime hours to cover their basic living costs", "Production could simply shift to countries with weaker environmental standards", "Stricter regulation would immediately eliminate all garment manufacturing jobs worldwide", "Extended producer responsibility has already been fully implemented in most countries"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has consumer behavior been slower to change than stated environmental concerns would predict?", options: ["Fast fashion remains more convenient and affordable than sustainable alternatives", "Retailers have introduced recycling programs and conscious clothing lines", "Consumers are entirely unaware of the industry's labor and environmental practices", "Sustainable alternatives are now cheaper than most fast-fashion garments"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a consequence of fast fashion's production model?", options: ["Severe water shortages entirely caused by cotton farming alone", "Persistently unsafe factory conditions despite retailer reform pledges", "A steady increase in the retail price of cotton garments worldwide", "Textile waste accumulating faster than recycling systems can process it"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"externalize\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["offload", "eliminate", "outsource", "exteriorize"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"undermine\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["destroy", "weaken", "regulate", "underline"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["purchasing habits", "stated attitudes", "critics' concerns", "retailers' recycling programs"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the relationship between consumer concern and industry reform, based on the passage?", options: ["Consumer concern has already driven most retailers to fully reform their supply chains", "Genuine change likely requires more than consumer attitudes shifting on their own", "Industry reform pledges caused a decline in consumer concern about fashion", "Extended producer responsibility requires manufacturers to help process discarded garments"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in presenting the debate between policymakers and industry representatives in paragraph 6?", options: ["To argue that all regulation of fast fashion is fundamentally misguided", "To celebrate the success of extended producer responsibility policies", "To show that regulatory solutions face real practical limits, not just resistance", "To prove conclusively that regulation cannot succeed under any circumstances"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Deciphering the Human Lifespan: Genetics vs. Lifestyle", paragraphs: ["Not only does research on human longevity keep expanding, but it has also repeatedly forced scientists to revise how much of a role genetics actually plays in determining lifespan. Twin studies, long treated as the gold standard for separating hereditary from environmental influence, have variously estimated that genetic factors account for somewhere between twenty and thirty percent of the variation in how long people live a range that, however precise it may sound, conceals considerable disagreement about methodology and population sampling. What remains largely uncontested, even amid this disagreement, is that genetics alone cannot explain why lifespan has increased so dramatically over the past century, a shift too rapid to be attributed to evolutionary change.", "Lifestyle factors, by contrast, appear to exert an influence that may rival or even exceed that of inherited genes, though researchers are careful to note that isolating any single behavior's contribution remains notoriously difficult given how diet, exercise, sleep, and stress interact with one another. Diets emphasizing whole foods and moderate caloric intake are frequently associated with longer, healthier lives, though the evidence linking specific dietary patterns to lifespan extension in humans, as opposed to laboratory animals, remains largely correlational rather than experimentally confirmed. Regular physical activity, similarly, correlates strongly with reduced mortality risk, yet the degree to which this relationship reflects a causal effect of exercise itself, rather than the fact that healthier individuals are more able to exercise in the first place, continues to divide researchers.", "That certain regions of the world display unusually high concentrations of centenarians has generated considerable scientific and popular interest, even though the explanations offered for these so-called blue zones remain contested. Communities in parts of Japan, Italy, and Costa Rica, among others, have been credited with dietary patterns, strong social ties, and continued physical activity into old age as explanations for their residents' longevity. Some researchers have more recently questioned whether inconsistent birth-record keeping in certain regions may have inflated reported ages, suggesting that at least part of the blue-zone phenomenon could reflect data quality rather than any genuine biological or lifestyle advantage.", "Epigenetics — the study of how environmental factors can alter gene expression without changing the underlying DNA sequence has added a further layer of complexity to the genetics-versus-lifestyle framing, since it suggests the two are not entirely separable categories to begin with. Chronic stress, poor nutrition, and exposure to certain toxins have all been shown, in various studies, to modify how genes associated with aging and disease are expressed, meaning that lifestyle choices may influence not only an individual's own health outcomes but potentially those of subsequent generations as well. Whether these epigenetic changes are reliably heritable in humans, however, remains a question that current research can only partially answer, given the ethical and practical constraints on studying multigenerational effects directly.", "Much of what is known about longevity determinants derives from observational studies, which can identify correlations but cannot, on their own, establish that a given factor causes longer life rather than merely accompanying it. Randomized controlled trials, the standard for establishing causation, are rarely feasible when the outcome of interest a longer lifespan may take decades to observe, forcing researchers to rely on proxy measures such as biomarkers of aging that may or may not track meaningfully with eventual longevity. Some scientists have proposed that advances in tracking biological rather than chronological age could eventually shorten the feedback loop between intervention and measurable outcome, though such tools remain, for now, more promising than proven.", "None of this uncertainty implies that lifestyle interventions are not worth pursuing; even modest, well-supported changes in diet and activity are associated with measurable reductions in the risk of major chronic diseases, regardless of how they ultimately affect maximum lifespan. It would, however, be an overstatement to claim that lifestyle changes alone can override a strong genetic predisposition toward particular conditions, just as it would be premature to conclude that genetics sets a fixed ceiling that no combination of behaviors could meaningfully approach. The more defensible position, most researchers suggest, is that genetics and lifestyle interact in ways that current research has only begun to map with any precision.", "Untangling exactly how much of human lifespan variation traces back to inherited genes versus accumulated choices may, in the end, prove less useful than understanding how the two interact across a person's life course. What does seem increasingly clear is that neither factor operates in isolation, and that public health strategies built on either genetic determinism or purely behavioral responsibility are likely to misrepresent a far more entangled reality."], questions: [{ q: "Which statement best reflects the passage's overall argument about the relationship between genetics and lifestyle?", options: ["Twin studies show genetics explain between twenty and thirty percent of longevity", "Genetics and lifestyle interact in complex ways that resist simple, separate measurement", "Lifestyle factors have been conclusively proven to matter more than genetics", "Public health should rely entirely on genetic screening to predict lifespan"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the main obstacle to establishing that exercise directly causes a longer life?", options: ["Healthier people may simply be more able to exercise in the first place", "Exercise has been shown to have no measurable effect on mortality risk at all", "Healthier individuals are entirely incapable of exercising more than others", "Researchers have fully confirmed that exercise causes reduced mortality risk"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What alternative explanation does the passage raise for the high number of long-lived people in blue zone regions?", options: ["Strong social ties and continued physical activity explain their longevity", "Inconsistent birth records may have inflated the reported ages of residents", "Randomized controlled trials have disproven all blue zone longevity claims", "Biomarkers of aging confirm that blue zone residents age more slowly"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on the passage, why can epigenetics not be neatly separated from the genetics-versus-lifestyle framing described earlier?", options: ["Environmental factors can alter how inherited genes are expressed", "Environmental factors always completely replace the function of inherited genes", "Epigenetic changes have been proven to pass reliably to every future generation", "Twin studies have shown that genetics explain most epigenetic variation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a limitation of current longevity research?", options: ["Randomized controlled trials are rarely feasible for studying lifespan directly", "It is entirely impossible to isolate any single lifestyle factor's contribution", "Genetic testing has completely replaced the need for observational studies", "Inconsistent record-keeping may distort reported ages in some regions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"notoriously\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["famously", "slightly", "biologically", "notably"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"entangled\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["confused", "intertwined", "inherited", "entailed"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["chronic diseases", "measurable reductions", "diet and activity changes", "maximum lifespan"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the authors' view of genetic determinism, based on the passage as a whole?", options: ["They believe genetics play no meaningful role in determining lifespan at all", "They regard genetic determinism as an oversimplified account of longevity", "Genetic determinism has caused researchers to abandon lifestyle-based interventions entirely", "Twin studies estimate genetics account for twenty to thirty percent of lifespan variation"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward claims that either genetics or lifestyle alone can explain human lifespan?", options: ["Dismissive specifically of blue-zone research as scientifically worthless", "Alarmed that epigenetic research threatens established genetic science", "Skeptical of either factor being treated as sufficient on its own", "Fully certain that genetics and lifestyle contribute in fixed, equal proportions"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-124", num: 124, title: "Đề 124", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Online Learning: Opportunities, Challenges, and the Future of Education", paragraphs: ["Over the past decade, online learning has transformed from a niche alternative into a mainstream educational option embraced by millions of students, working professionals, and lifelong learners around the world. The rapid expansion of high-speed internet access, combined with the development of sophisticated digital platforms, has made it possible for anyone with a connected device to access courses, lectures, and qualifications that were previously available only to those who could attend a physical institution. This shift has been celebrated by many educators and policymakers as a democratising force — one that has the potential to reduce educational inequality and open doors that geography, cost, or personal circumstance might otherwise keep firmly shut.", "One of the most frequently cited advantages of online learning is its flexibility. Unlike traditional classroom-based education, which requires students to be present at a fixed location at a fixed time, online courses allow learners to study at their own pace and on their own schedule. This is particularly valuable for adults who are managing work and family responsibilities alongside their studies, as well as for students in rural or remote areas who may not have easy access to schools or universities. Many platforms also offer a wide variety of subjects, from basic literacy and numeracy to advanced professional certifications and university-level degrees, meaning that learners can tailor their educational journey to their specific needs and goals.", "Despite these advantages, online learning presents a number of significant challenges that educators and institutions are still working to address. One of the most persistent concerns is the issue of student engagement. Research consistently shows that online learners are considerably more likely to abandon a course before completing it compared to students enrolled in face-to-face programmes. Without the social pressure of a classroom environment, the encouragement of peers, and the immediate accountability that comes from a teacher's presence, many students find it difficult to maintain motivation over extended periods. This problem is particularly acute for younger learners and for those who are new to independent study.", "Access and equity present another layer of complexity. While online learning is often described as universally accessible, this claim obscures the reality that meaningful participation requires not only a device and an internet connection but also a quiet space to study, reliable electricity, and a sufficient level of digital literacy. In many parts of the world — and even within wealthy countries — these conditions cannot be taken for granted. Studies conducted during the COVID-19 pandemic, when schools worldwide shifted abruptly to online delivery, revealed stark disparities in learning outcomes between students from higher-income households equipped with dedicated computers and fast broadband and those from lower-income families sharing a single smartphone with limited data.", "The quality of online instruction has also attracted considerable scrutiny. Critics argue that not all online courses are created equal — that the market has been flooded with low-quality content produced by unqualified providers seeking to capitalise on the growing demand for digital education. Reputable institutions such as universities and established professional training bodies have responded by investing heavily in the development of well-designed online programmes, employing instructional designers, video producers, and learning technology specialists to create content that is both engaging and pedagogically sound. Accreditation bodies in many countries have also begun to develop frameworks for evaluating and certifying the quality of online credentials, though these systems remain inconsistent across different educational contexts and national boundaries.", "Looking ahead, most experts agree that the future of education will be neither purely online nor purely face-to-face but rather a thoughtfully designed blend of both. The concept of hybrid or blended learning — in which students engage with digital content independently and then use classroom time for discussion, collaboration, and hands-on activities — has gained considerable traction in schools, universities, and corporate training programmes worldwide. Rather than viewing online and traditional learning as competing alternatives, educators increasingly regard them as complementary tools that, when combined intelligently, can offer students a richer and more flexible learning experience than either approach could provide alone."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Online learning is most effective when it is used exclusively by adult professionals who already possess strong independent study skills.", "Online learning has completely replaced traditional classroom education and is now the preferred method of study in most countries.", "The primary problem with online learning is that most digital courses are produced by unqualified providers and therefore lack academic credibility.", "Online learning offers significant benefits in terms of flexibility and access but also presents challenges related to engagement, equity, and quality."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, who benefits most from the flexibility that online learning provides?", options: ["Students who prefer a competitive academic environment and want to compare their progress directly with that of their classmates.", "University professors who wish to reduce the amount of time they spend delivering lectures in traditional classroom settings.", "Young children who need a structured and supervised learning environment in order to develop foundational academic skills.", "Adults who need to balance their studies with existing work and family commitments or who live in remote areas."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the most persistent challenge associated with online learning?", options: ["The high cost of enrolling in reputable online programmes offered by established universities and professional training institutions.", "The inability of online platforms to deliver practical or laboratory-based components of science and engineering qualifications.", "The difficulty of maintaining student motivation over time without the social pressure and accountability of a classroom setting.", "The lack of variety in the subjects and qualifications available through online platforms compared to traditional institutions."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did studies conducted during the COVID-19 pandemic reveal about online learning?", options: ["That students who studied online during the pandemic performed significantly better than those who attended physical schools.", "That younger students adapted more quickly to online learning than older students because of their familiarity with digital technology.", "That there were significant differences in learning outcomes between students with good equipment and those without adequate resources.", "That the sudden move to online delivery caused most teachers to abandon digital tools and return to printed materials."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["hybrid and blended learning programmes that combine independent digital study with collaborative classroom-based activities.", "the educators and policymakers who advocate for the integration of digital tools into mainstream educational practice.", "the students who participate in both online and face-to-face learning programmes as part of a blended educational model.", "online learning and traditional face-to-face learning, which are increasingly viewed as complementary rather than competing approaches."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have accreditation bodies responded to the growth of online education?", options: ["They have begun developing frameworks to evaluate and certify the quality of online credentials, though these remain inconsistent across contexts.", "They have partnered directly with technology companies to create a single international standard for online educational qualifications.", "They have banned unaccredited providers from operating online platforms and restricted digital credentials to established universities only.", "They have required all online course providers to employ instructional designers and video producers before receiving official recognition."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"motivation\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the inner drive and determination that a person needs to continue pursuing a goal over a sustained period.", "the structured timetable and routine that helps students organise their study time in a productive and efficient way.", "the external rewards and penalties that teachers use to encourage students to complete assignments on time.", "the academic ability and prior knowledge that a learner brings to a new course or educational programme."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the schools and universities that shifted abruptly to online delivery when physical campuses were closed during the pandemic.", "the stark disparities in learning outcomes that were identified by researchers studying the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic.", "the dedicated computers and fast broadband connections available to students from higher-income households during the pandemic.", "the students from lower-income families who shared a single device with limited data access during the school closures."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a condition required for meaningful participation in online learning?", options: ["A quiet and dedicated space that allows the learner to study without significant distraction or interruption.", "A sufficient level of digital literacy to navigate online platforms and engage effectively with digital course content.", "Access to a device capable of connecting to the internet and running the required educational software or platform.", "A formal academic qualification that demonstrates the learner has met the prerequisites for the course they wish to study."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's overall view of online learning based on the passage?", options: ["The author is enthusiastic about online learning and argues that its advantages clearly outweigh any challenges it currently presents.", "The author believes online learning is fundamentally inferior to face-to-face instruction and should only be used as a last resort.", "The author is deeply skeptical of online learning and implies that most digital courses fail to meet acceptable academic standards.", "The author takes a cautiously optimistic view, recognising both the genuine potential and the real limitations of online learning."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Air Pollution in India: Causes, Consequences, and the Search for Solutions", paragraphs: ["India is home to some of the most polluted cities on Earth. According to data published by the World Health Organisation and various independent air quality monitoring agencies, a substantial proportion of the world's cities with the highest concentrations of fine particulate matter — known as PM2.5 — are located on the Indian subcontinent, with cities such as Delhi, Kanpur, and Patna consistently appearing near the top of global rankings. The scale of the crisis is difficult to overstate: air pollution is now recognised as one of the leading contributors to premature death and chronic illness in India, responsible for an estimated 1.6 million deaths annually and a significant reduction in average life expectancy across the country.", "The sources of India's air pollution are diverse and deeply interconnected with the country's patterns of economic development, energy production, and agricultural practice. Vehicular emissions from the country's rapidly expanding fleet of cars, trucks, motorcycles, and auto-rickshaws — many of which run on older, less fuel-efficient engines — contribute substantially to urban air quality degradation. Industrial activity, including manufacturing, mining, brick kilns, and thermal power generation fuelled predominantly by coal, releases large quantities of sulphur dioxide, nitrogen oxides, and particulate matter into the atmosphere. In rural areas, the combustion of solid biomass fuels such as wood, crop residues, and dried animal dung for cooking and heating constitutes a major and frequently underestimated source of indoor and outdoor pollution alike.", "One of the most dramatic and well-publicised contributors to seasonal pollution spikes in northern India is the practice of crop residue burning. Following the harvest of rice and wheat crops in the states of Punjab and Haryana, farmers burn the remaining stubble in their fields to clear the land rapidly before the next planting season. This practice, while economically rational from the perspective of individual farmers who face tight timelines and limited access to mechanised alternatives, releases enormous quantities of smoke, particulate matter, and toxic gases into the atmosphere over a period of several weeks. The resulting pollution plumes frequently travel hundreds of kilometres, blanketing Delhi and the surrounding National Capital Region in a dense haze that sends air quality indices soaring to hazardous levels and forces schools and construction sites to temporarily close.", "The health consequences of sustained exposure to elevated levels of air pollution are well documented and deeply alarming. Fine particulate matter of 2.5 micrometres or less in diameter is particularly insidious because it is small enough to bypass the body's natural respiratory defences, penetrating deep into the lungs and, in some cases, entering the bloodstream. Chronic exposure has been linked to a range of serious conditions including respiratory diseases such as asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, cardiovascular disorders, and a heightened risk of certain cancers. Children are disproportionately affected because their lungs are still developing, and studies conducted in Delhi have found that a significant proportion of schoolchildren in the city already show measurable signs of reduced lung function. Elderly individuals and those with pre-existing health conditions face similarly elevated risks.", "Government responses to the air pollution crisis have been varied in both ambition and effectiveness. The National Clean Air Programme, launched by the Indian government in 2019, set a target of reducing PM2.5 and PM10 concentrations by between 20 and 30 percent in the most polluted cities by 2024. Measures undertaken as part of this programme include the accelerated phase-out of older, more polluting vehicles, the promotion of compressed natural gas as an alternative fuel for public transport, the tightening of emission standards for industries, and investments in urban green space. However, implementation has been uneven, enforcement of pollution regulations remains patchy in many states, and critics argue that the targets set were insufficiently ambitious given the severity of the problem.", "Longer-term solutions will require not merely regulatory action but a fundamental restructuring of India's energy system and agricultural sector. The transition away from coal-fired power generation toward renewable energy sources such as solar and wind power is already underway, and India has made ambitious commitments at international climate forums to expand its renewable energy capacity substantially. In the agricultural sector, incentive schemes to encourage farmers to adopt alternatives to crop burning — including the provision of subsidised machinery to process stubble in situ — have shown promise in some regions, though uptake has been limited by financial constraints and longstanding habits of practice. Addressing air pollution in India ultimately demands a combination of technological innovation, policy coherence, and sustained political will at both state and national levels.", "The human cost of inaction is measured not only in mortality statistics but in the daily quality of life of hundreds of millions of people who breathe polluted air from birth. For India to fulfil its aspiration of becoming a prosperous and equitable society, tackling the air pollution crisis is not merely an environmental imperative but a fundamental matter of public health and social justice."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To describe the specific meteorological conditions that cause pollution levels in northern India to spike during winter months.", "To compare air pollution levels in Indian cities with those in other developing countries and identify which nation faces the most serious challenge.", "To argue that the Indian government has failed entirely in its efforts to reduce air pollution and must adopt a completely new policy approach.", "To examine the causes, health consequences, and policy responses associated with the air pollution crisis in India."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what makes PM2.5 particles particularly dangerous to human health?", options: ["They are too small to be filtered out by the body's natural defences and can penetrate deeply into the lungs and bloodstream.", "They remain suspended in the atmosphere for longer than larger particles, meaning that exposure continues even after the original source has stopped.", "They react chemically with nitrogen oxides in the atmosphere to form compounds that are significantly more toxic than their individual components.", "They are produced exclusively by coal-fired power plants and therefore tend to concentrate in industrial areas where most workers live."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do farmers in Punjab and Haryana burn crop residues despite the environmental consequences?", options: ["Because mechanised alternatives to burning have been repeatedly shown to damage the soil structure in the flat plains of northern India.", "Because burning is the only method approved by state agricultural authorities for clearing fields between the rice and wheat planting seasons.", "Because the ash produced by burning stubble significantly enriches the soil and increases the yield of the following season's crop.", "Because it is an economically practical way to clear their fields quickly given the tight planting schedule and limited access to machinery."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"habits\" in paragraph 6 refers most closely to _______.", options: ["the financial management strategies that smallholder farmers rely on to minimise costs during periods of low agricultural income.", "the seasonal patterns of energy consumption in rural households that contribute to elevated levels of indoor air pollution.", "the general cultural traditions and dietary customs that shape the daily routines of farming communities across rural India.", "the longstanding practice of burning crop residues that farmers have continued despite the availability of subsidised machinery."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the target set by India's National Clean Air Programme launched in 2019?", options: ["To eliminate PM2.5 and PM10 pollution entirely from India's ten most polluted cities within five years of the programme's launch.", "To transition all public transport vehicles in major Indian cities to compressed natural gas within three years of the programme's introduction.", "To plant sufficient urban green space in polluted cities to offset at least half of the particulate matter generated by industrial activity.", "To reduce concentrations of PM2.5 and PM10 by between 20 and 30 percent in the most polluted cities by the year 2024."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 6, what can be understood about the relationship between children's health and the agricultural sector?", options: ["The seasonal pollution spikes caused by crop burning contribute to the lung damage already documented in schoolchildren in cities like Delhi.", "Studies have found that children raised in farming communities develop stronger respiratory systems due to early exposure to outdoor environments.", "Children's lung development is primarily affected by indoor pollution from biomass fuel use rather than by outdoor agricultural burning.", "Children in rural agricultural regions are less exposed to air pollution than urban children because they live farther from industrial facilities."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"insidious\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually harmful in a way that is not immediately apparent, making the damage difficult to recognise until it has become serious.", "acutely painful, causing intense discomfort that prompts affected individuals to seek medical attention without delay.", "immediately detectable, producing visible symptoms that allow doctors to diagnose and treat the condition at an early stage.", "highly contagious, spreading rapidly from one person to another through direct physical contact or shared air in confined spaces."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"combustion\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the chemical treatment of solid fuels using industrial solvents to extract usable energy without producing open flames.", "the mechanical processing of agricultural waste using specialised equipment designed to reduce its volume efficiently.", "the process of burning a material, during which it reacts with oxygen and releases heat, light, and airborne pollutants.", "the gradual decomposition of organic materials through the action of bacteria and moisture over an extended period."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure included in India's National Clean Air Programme?", options: ["Making investments in urban green spaces as part of a broader strategy to improve air quality in heavily polluted cities.", "Introducing a national tax on coal consumption by industrial facilities to fund the development of renewable energy infrastructure.", "Encouraging the use of compressed natural gas as an alternative fuel source for buses and other forms of public transport.", "Speeding up the removal of older and more polluting vehicles from roads in cities with the worst air quality."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the effectiveness of single-sector policy interventions in addressing India's air pollution crisis?", options: ["Single-sector interventions have already achieved measurable reductions in PM2.5 levels, suggesting that continued focus on one area at a time is the correct strategy.", "Single-sector interventions targeting agriculture have proven more effective than those targeting industry because crop burning is the dominant source of pollution.", "Single-sector interventions are the most practical approach because they allow resources to be concentrated on the most significant individual pollution source.", "Single-sector interventions are likely to be insufficient because the problem requires coordinated action across energy, agriculture, transport, and governance simultaneously."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Tobacco Consumption in China: Scale, Culture, and the Struggle for Reform", paragraphs: ["China's relationship with tobacco is, by almost any measure, extraordinary in its scale. The country is home to roughly 300 million smokers — nearly a third of all smokers on the planet — and accounts for approximately 40 percent of global cigarette consumption. [1] Each year, Chinese smokers consume an estimated 2.3 trillion cigarettes, a figure that dwarfs the output of every other tobacco market in the world. The human cost of this consumption is correspondingly vast: smoking-related illnesses kill an estimated one million Chinese citizens annually, a toll that public health experts warn will rise dramatically in the coming decades as the full consequences of decades of heavy smoking manifest in the country's ageing population.", "To understand why tobacco use remains so deeply entrenched in China, it is necessary to examine the cultural and social frameworks within which smoking is embedded. In many parts of Chinese society, the offering and sharing of cigarettes functions as a powerful social ritual — a gesture of hospitality, respect, and solidarity that is particularly pronounced in male-dominated professional and social environments. Refusing a cigarette offered by a colleague, superior, or business partner can be perceived as a social slight, and in contexts where maintaining harmonious interpersonal relationships is of paramount importance, many individuals find themselves smoking not from personal desire but from an unwillingness to disrupt social bonds. This deeply normalised role of tobacco in social interaction has made behavioural change considerably more difficult to achieve than in societies where smoking is primarily an individual habit with fewer communal dimensions.", "The political economy of tobacco in China adds a further layer of complexity to the reform challenge. The China National Tobacco Corporation (CNTC) is a state-owned enterprise that simultaneously functions as the world's largest tobacco company and the regulatory body responsible for overseeing the tobacco industry it profits from. This structural conflict of interest means that the agency nominally charged with enforcing tobacco control measures has a direct financial incentive to maximise cigarette sales rather than reduce them. The CNTC contributes a substantial proportion of national tax revenue — at times exceeding ten percent of total government income — which creates powerful institutional resistance to policies that might significantly curtail tobacco consumption. Critics have long argued that meaningful reform is structurally impossible so long as the same entity both regulates and benefits from the industry. [2]", "China did accede to the World Health Organisation's Framework Convention on Tobacco Control (FCTC) in 2005, committing itself in principle to a comprehensive set of evidence-based tobacco control measures. However, the translation of these commitments into domestic policy has been markedly incomplete. Smoke-free legislation, for example, has been introduced in several major cities including Beijing and Shanghai, but enforcement has been inconsistent and penalties for violations remain relatively modest compared to those applied in countries with more robust tobacco control records. Graphic health warnings on cigarette packaging, which research consistently identifies as one of the most cost-effective tools for reducing tobacco uptake among young people, cover a smaller proportion of pack surfaces in China than the FCTC recommends and lack the explicit imagery found on packets in many other signatory countries. [3]", "Despite these structural obstacles, there are tentative signs that attitudes toward smoking in China are beginning to shift, particularly among younger and more urban segments of the population. Awareness of the health risks associated with smoking has grown substantially, driven partly by improved public health education and partly by the increasing integration of Chinese consumers into global information flows through social media and international travel. Surveys conducted in major metropolitan areas suggest that smoking rates among younger cohorts are lower than those recorded for older generations, and that non-smoking is increasingly regarded as compatible with — and in some professional circles even emblematic of — a modern, health-conscious lifestyle. [4]", "The trajectory of tobacco control in China will have consequences that extend well beyond its borders. Given China's dominance of global tobacco markets, even a modest reduction in domestic consumption would represent a significant public health achievement on a global scale. International health organisations and bilateral partners continue to advocate for stronger enforcement of existing smoke-free legislation, larger and more graphic health warnings, and the structural separation of tobacco regulation from tobacco commerce. Whether the Chinese government proves willing to prioritise long-term public health over short-term fiscal considerations remains the defining question for what is arguably the world's most consequential tobacco control challenge.", "Ultimately, the story of smoking in China illustrates a broader tension that many rapidly developing societies face: the conflict between inherited cultural practices and economic structures that benefit powerful institutional actors on one hand, and the mounting evidence of preventable suffering that those practices and structures sustain on the other."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["China's failure to ratify the World Health Organisation's Framework Convention on Tobacco Control and the diplomatic consequences of that decision.", "The extraordinary scale of tobacco consumption in China and the cultural, political, and structural factors that make meaningful reform exceptionally difficult.", "The role of social media and international travel in transforming the attitudes of young Chinese consumers toward smoking and personal health.", "The financial dependence of the Chinese government on tobacco tax revenue and the likelihood that this dependence will increase in coming decades."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do many individuals in China continue to smoke even when they do not personally wish to?", options: ["Because public health campaigns in China have not yet succeeded in communicating the health risks of secondhand smoke to the general population.", "Because nicotine addiction developed during adolescence makes it physiologically impossible for most smokers to quit without medical assistance.", "Because cigarettes are heavily subsidised by the state-owned tobacco corporation, making them significantly cheaper than other consumer goods.", "Because refusing a cigarette in professional or social contexts can be interpreted as disrespectful and damaging to important relationships."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"themselves\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the male-dominated professional environments in which the sharing of cigarettes is most strongly normalised as a social practice.", "the colleagues, superiors, and business partners who offer cigarettes as a gesture of hospitality in professional social settings.", "the many individuals who find themselves smoking due to social pressure rather than any personal desire to consume tobacco.", "the public health experts who have studied the relationship between social rituals and tobacco consumption in Chinese society."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what structural problem does the China National Tobacco Corporation represent?", options: ["It is the world's largest tobacco company but lacks the technical expertise and research capacity to develop less harmful cigarette products.", "It operates as a private monopoly that deliberately suppresses competition from domestic and international tobacco producers to maximise profit.", "It simultaneously acts as the industry's largest commercial beneficiary and as the regulatory authority responsible for controlling that same industry.", "It prioritises exports to international markets over domestic supply, which has created shortages and driven up cigarette prices for Chinese consumers."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, in what way has China's implementation of FCTC commitments been inadequate?", options: ["Chinese authorities have actively promoted tobacco consumption in rural areas to offset declining sales in major urban centres.", "China has refused to introduce any smoke-free legislation in public spaces, in direct violation of its obligations as a signatory to the treaty.", "China has failed to establish any system of taxation on tobacco products despite the FCTC's requirement that all signatories do so.", "Health warnings on Chinese cigarette packets cover less surface area and lack the explicit imagery required under FCTC recommendations."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 4, what can be concluded about the principal obstacles to effective tobacco control in China?", options: ["The main obstacles are scientific, as Chinese researchers have not yet developed sufficiently persuasive evidence of tobacco's health harms for domestic audiences.", "The primary obstacle is public resistance, as the majority of Chinese citizens actively oppose government interference in personal lifestyle choices.", "The central obstacle is international pressure, which has pushed the Chinese government to adopt tobacco control measures that are culturally inappropriate.", "The key obstacles are structural and institutional, combining a regulator with commercial interests in the industry and inconsistent enforcement of existing laws."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"conflict\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a situation in which an entity has competing interests that compromise its ability to act impartially in fulfilling its responsibilities.", "a legal dispute between the state-owned tobacco corporation and international health organisations over the interpretation of treaty obligations.", "a political confrontation between advocates of tobacco reform and legislators who depend on tobacco industry funding for their campaigns.", "a disagreement between government ministries over which agency should be granted authority to regulate the domestic tobacco industry."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"entrenched\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["officially sanctioned by government policy in a way that makes it legally protected from regulation or public health intervention.", "openly contested by a growing number of people who regard the practice as incompatible with modern values and aspirations.", "so deeply and firmly established within a social or institutional context that it is highly resistant to change or elimination.", "spreading rapidly through a population in a way that overwhelms the capacity of public health systems to respond effectively."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a tobacco control measure that China has introduced or been criticised for implementing inadequately?", options: ["A complete nationwide ban on tobacco advertising across all media platforms, including digital and social media channels.", "Accession to the World Health Organisation's Framework Convention on Tobacco Control, which China signed in 2005.", "The introduction of smoke-free legislation in major cities, though enforcement of these laws has been described as inconsistent.", "The placement of health warnings on cigarette packaging, though these are considered insufficient in size and lacking in explicit imagery."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This arrangement is widely regarded by public health advocates as a fundamental impediment to genuine tobacco reform.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Africa's Development Paradox: Resources, Institutions, and the Contested Path to Prosperity", paragraphs: ["Africa occupies a paradoxical position in the global imagination and in the empirical literature of development economics. The continent possesses an extraordinary endowment of natural resources — an estimated 30 percent of the world's mineral reserves, vast tracts of arable land, the largest concentration of megafauna on Earth, and a demographic profile characterised by a youthful and rapidly growing population that many analysts regard as a potential engine of future economic dynamism. [1] Yet despite these structural advantages, sub-Saharan Africa remains the region with the highest concentration of extreme poverty globally, and the gap between its resource wealth and the material welfare of its citizens has generated one of the most contested and consequential debates in contemporary development thought.", "The dominant explanatory framework in academic and policy circles for much of the late twentieth century was the so-called \"resource curse\" hypothesis — the counterintuitive proposition that an abundance of natural resources tends to impede rather than accelerate economic development. Proponents of this view point to a consistent empirical pattern: resource-rich African states such as Angola, Nigeria, and the Democratic Republic of Congo have, over several decades, recorded growth trajectories that compare unfavourably with less resource-endowed neighbours, even when controlling for other variables. The theoretical mechanisms proposed to explain this association include the Dutch Disease effect, whereby resource export revenues cause currency appreciation that erodes the competitiveness of non-resource sectors; the tendency of resource rents to finance patronage networks rather than productive public investment; and the weakening of state-society fiscal contracts that occurs when governments derive revenue primarily from extraction rather than taxation of citizens.", "Critics of the resource curse thesis, however, argue that the causal story is considerably more nuanced than its proponents acknowledge. Botswana, for example, is frequently cited as a counter-case: a diamond-dependent economy that nonetheless achieved sustained, broad-based growth over several decades by investing resource revenues in public goods, maintaining institutional quality, and diversifying economic activity beyond the extractive sector. The divergence between Botswana and countries such as Angola or the DRC, these critics contend, suggests that it is not resource wealth per se that determines developmental outcomes but rather the quality of the institutions through which those resources are governed. This institutionalist perspective — associated with scholars such as Daron Acemoglu and James Robinson — locates the roots of Africa's development challenges not in geography or resource endowment but in the extractive colonial institutions that were deliberately constructed to maximise the transfer of value from African territories to metropolitan powers, and whose legacies continue to shape governance structures across the continent.", "The colonial legacy argument is itself subject to significant scholarly contestation. While few serious analysts dispute that colonial extraction caused profound and lasting damage — dismantling pre-existing trade networks, subordinating African economies to the needs of European industrialisation, and establishing administrative structures oriented toward control rather than welfare — the degree to which contemporary governance failures can be attributed to colonial inheritance rather than post-independence political choices remains genuinely unresolved. Some scholars point to the extraordinary diversity of post-independence trajectories across countries that shared broadly similar colonial experiences, arguing that this variation is more readily explained by the quality of specific leadership decisions, regional geopolitical dynamics, and the nature of Cold War patronage relationships than by colonial-era institutional legacies alone. [2]", "The question of external agency — specifically, the role of foreign aid, international financial institutions, and more recently Chinese investment — in shaping African development outcomes has generated equally fierce debate. Proponents of aid pessimism, most prominently associated with the economist Dambisa Moyo, argue that decades of official development assistance have created aid dependency, undermined domestic revenue mobilisation, and perversely strengthened the hand of unaccountable elites by providing governments with resources that insulate them from the fiscal pressures that might otherwise compel accountability to citizens. Defenders of well-designed aid programmes counter that the empirical evidence is considerably more mixed, pointing to measurable progress in health outcomes, educational attainment, and institutional capacity in countries where aid has been effectively targeted and conditioned.", "China's rapidly expanding economic presence in Africa — encompassing infrastructure financing, resource extraction concessions, and a growing volume of consumer goods trade — has added a new dimension to these longstanding debates. Optimistic assessments emphasise the infrastructure gap that Chinese investment is helping to close and the absence of the political conditionalities that have made Western aid relationships fraught with sovereignty concerns. More critical perspectives highlight evidence of debt distress in some recipient countries, the limited transfer of skills and technology to African workers in Chinese-financed projects, and the potential for new forms of resource dependency to reproduce, in modified form, the structural vulnerabilities that characterised earlier colonial relationships. [3]", "What most analysts across these divergent perspectives share is a recognition that the developmental challenges facing Africa are not reducible to a single cause or addressable through a single policy instrument. The continent's diversity — encompassing fifty-four sovereign states, hundreds of distinct ethnic and linguistic communities, vastly different resource profiles, and radically different institutional trajectories — resists the kind of monolithic analysis that has too often characterised external commentary on African affairs. [4] Progress, where it has occurred, has tended to emerge from the alignment of capable domestic institutions, accountable political leadership, and external relationships structured to support rather than extract — a combination that remains unevenly distributed but is far from absent across the African continent."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the author's approach to explaining Africa's development challenges?", options: ["The author presents multiple contested explanatory frameworks without endorsing any one as definitively correct, emphasising the complexity of the problem.", "The author advances the resource curse thesis as the single most persuasive explanation and systematically rebuts competing frameworks.", "The author concludes that external actors such as foreign aid donors and Chinese investors bear the greatest responsibility for Africa's persistent poverty.", "The author argues that colonial institutional legacies are the primary cause of Africa's development failures and that other explanations are secondary."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is identified as a theoretical mechanism through which natural resource wealth may impede development?", options: ["Resource export revenues can cause currency appreciation that reduces the international competitiveness of other economic sectors.", "Resource revenues allow governments to invest heavily in education and infrastructure, creating expectations of public services that are unsustainable after reserves are depleted.", "The global commodities market exposes resource-dependent economies to price volatility that discourages foreign investors from financing non-extractive industries.", "Resource wealth attracts disproportionate foreign direct investment into the extractive sector, crowding out domestic entrepreneurship in manufacturing and agriculture."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to the idea that _______.", options: ["the extractive colonial institutions constructed by European powers continue to determine economic trajectories in Africa more than any post-independence variable.", "the quality of governing institutions rather than the mere possession of natural resources is the decisive factor shaping developmental outcomes.", "Botswana's diamond-dependent economy has demonstrated that resource wealth can consistently support broad-based development when revenues are reinvested effectively.", "the resource curse hypothesis has been sufficiently disproven by counter-cases to warrant its removal from mainstream development economics literature."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is Botswana frequently cited in debates about the resource curse?", options: ["Because it is the only African country to have successfully eliminated extreme poverty entirely through the strategic management of diamond revenues.", "Because its experience demonstrates that resource wealth can support sustained development when accompanied by strong institutions and productive public investment.", "Because it represents a case where external aid from Western donors was successfully used to offset the negative effects of commodity price volatility.", "Because its government deliberately chose to extract resources slowly over several generations rather than maximising output during periods of high global demand."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 5, what does the passage suggest about the relationship between colonial history and contemporary governance in Africa?", options: ["Colonial history is the sole determinant of governance quality in Africa, and post-independence political choices have had no meaningful independent effect on development outcomes.", "Colonial history created strong and capable administrative structures in some African countries that subsequent governments have failed to maintain or build upon effectively.", "Colonial history is largely irrelevant to understanding contemporary African governance, as the diversity of post-independence outcomes proves that inherited institutions were not determinative.", "Colonial history caused demonstrable damage to African institutions, but the extent to which it explains current failures relative to post-independence decisions remains genuinely debated."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do critics raise about China's economic engagement in Africa?", options: ["That the political conditionalities attached to Chinese financing have enabled Beijing to exert undue influence over African domestic policy decisions.", "That Chinese consumer goods imports have destroyed Africa's nascent manufacturing sector by undercutting locally produced goods on price.", "That Chinese investment has been concentrated exclusively in coastal urban centres, deepening regional inequality within African countries.", "That Chinese-financed projects have generated debt pressures and limited skills transfer, potentially creating new forms of structural dependency."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"undermined\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporarily disrupted the normal functioning of a system in ways that were quickly identified and corrected.", "completely destroyed the institutional foundations of something in a sudden and irreversible manner.", "gradually weakened or eroded the effectiveness of something that would otherwise have functioned more robustly.", "indirectly supported the development of something by removing obstacles that had previously prevented its growth."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"proposed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["put forward as theoretical explanations that remain subject to scholarly debate rather than established as confirmed causal facts.", "definitively proven through rigorous empirical research conducted across multiple African economies over several decades.", "rejected by mainstream economists but preserved in academic literature as historically significant though empirically unsupported hypotheses.", "officially endorsed by international development institutions as the correct explanation for resource-related developmental failures."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a criticism directed at China's economic engagement in Africa?", options: ["Chinese investment risks creating new patterns of resource dependency that resemble, in modified form, earlier colonial economic relationships.", "Chinese-financed projects have provided limited opportunities for African workers to acquire new technical skills or access advanced technology.", "Chinese state-owned enterprises have been accused of systematically bribing African government officials to secure resource extraction concessions.", "Some recipient countries have experienced debt difficulties as a result of the financing arrangements associated with Chinese infrastructure projects."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Indeed, the variation in outcomes among countries sharing nearly identical colonial histories poses one of the most difficult challenges for any monocausal account of African underdevelopment.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
